# Indonesian names a weekday with the classifier "hari" in front of it: KBBI
# defines Senin as "hari ke-2 dalam jangka waktu satu minggu" and every other
# day name the same way, so "hari Senin" is one name and not the noun "hari"
# followed by something else (KBBI, Badan Pengembangan dan Pembinaan Bahasa).
# Listing the classifier form as a surface is what keeps "setiap hari Senin"
# from being read as "setiap hari" (every day) with the weekday discarded;
# bare "setiap hari" still means daily, because both readings are real.
senin
hari senin
